title: '2026-11-13: The procedure for planning a request is one page, with Jeff''s addition on judging complexity in its step 4.'
pid: b57212ce-da71-4bb2-914b-9e2abddc3b14
body: |2-
  It is [[id:6b88547d-78a8-4a64-a7e7-b754b201d97a][Plan a request only when a wrong start would cost more than the plan]].
  Behind it: the 14 sources, where it departs from them, and six doubts
  with the smallest trial for each, all linked from its body.
  What I am least sure of: whether a planner counts its own plan's
  signs honestly, and whether a builder can fold in Tay's and Libby's
  readings without a revision mission. Both are untried here, and no
  source describes a team that keeps nothing but notes and works one
  mind at a time.
  What surprised me: the best evidence for the review step is not on
  the web but in Tay's graph, her reading of your subscription design,
  which turned eight questions for Jeff into one. Step 5 copies it.
  Libby has no notes yet, so the reading step 5 asks of her is untried.
  Writing the brief for a plan's readers, and checking that Libby can
  do her part, might suit Libby herself on her first mission.
  I did not commit.
